# Capture frames are committed only from a gateValid run (see
# docs/captures/README.md). A software-rasteriser set is produced, looked at,
# and discarded — it would seed the §8 critic with fabricated evidence and
# spend tens of megabytes in a repo whose second invariant is that the bytes
# are few. Force-add the frames from a real-GPU run:
#     git add -f docs/captures/M0/*.png
docs/captures/**/*.png

# A glimpse is not a capture, and the whole directory is ignored rather than
# only its frames. `tools/glimpse.js` renders with the quality knobs
# deliberately wrecked — a hundredth of the grass, one segment per blade — so
# that a software rasteriser can produce a frame at all, and it stamps
# `gateValid: false` on every number it reports. Its JSON would be the more
# dangerous half to keep: a PNG that looks wrong is self-evidently wrong, and a
# committed statistic looks like a measurement of the thing we ship. It is an
# instrument reading, taken and discarded, exactly as the note above intends.
docs/captures/glimpse/

# The dev instruments (tools/*.js) drive Playwright, which some machines have
# globally and some do not. A local `npm i playwright` is how you get a capture
# out of a container that has the browsers but not the module — and it must
# never reach the repo. §2.2 keeps the *runtime* dependency-free and
# `python3 -m http.server 8080` sufficient forever; a node_modules committed
# here would read as a walk-back of that, and it is not one.
node_modules/
package.json
package-lock.json

# Throwaway probes at the repo root. A probe that reads the app's own modules
# has to sit where node will resolve `./src/...` from — the repo root — so it
# cannot live in a scratch directory the way the rest of the working files do.
# That is a real constraint rather than laziness: the point of a probe is to
# measure the shipped module, not a copy of it, and an import map only resolves
# against the served tree.
#
# So they land here, and they are never a deliverable. Anything a probe proves
# belongs in `tools/verify.js` as a check with the argument written out; a probe
# that turned out to be worth keeping gets a name and a home under `tools/`.
_*.mjs
critic_*.mjs
perfprobe*.mjs
